          "type": "singleCorrect",
          "level": 2,
          "question": {
            "text": "Ge (II) compounds are powerful reducing agents whereas $\\mathrm{Pb}$ (IV) compounds are strong oxidants. It can be because",
            "image": null
          },
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "x6WH0",
              "text": "\r\n$\\mathrm{Pb}$ is more electropositive than $\\mathrm{Ge}$.\r\n",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "L2OyP",
              "text": "\r\nionisation potential of lead is less than that of Ge.\r\n",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "FxFGE",
              "text": "\r\nionic radii of $\\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Pb}^{4+}$ are larger than that of $\\mathrm{Ge}^{2+}$ and $\\mathrm{Ge}^{4+}$\r\n",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "uuYqk",
              "text": "\r\nmore pronounced inert pair effect in lead has.",
              "isCorrect": true,
              "image": null
            }
          ],
          "correctValue": null,
          "solution": {
            "text": "Inert pair effect is more pronounced in heavier members like Pb.\r\nHence, $\\mathrm{Pb}$ (IV) compounds act as strong oxidising agents and are reduced to more stable $\\mathrm{Pb}$ (II) compounds.",
            "image": null
          },
          "videoSolution": null,
